answer:
If \(f\) and \(h\) are differentiable at a point \(x\), then the limits defining \(f'(x)\) and \(h'(x)\) exist.  The sum \(f+h\) has derivative\n\[
(f+h)'(x)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(x+h)+h(x+h)-f(x)-h(x)}{h}
          =\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}
           +\lim_{h\to0}\frac{h(x+h)-h(x)}{h}
          =f'(x)+h'(x).
\]\nBecause both \(f'(x)\) and \(h'(x)\) exist, their sum exists, so \((f+h)'(x)\) exists.  Thus \(f+h\) is differentiable at every point where \(f\) and \(h\) are differentiable, i.e., everywhere if both functions are differentiable everywhere.  This reasoning uses the linearity of limits and the fact that differentiability implies continuity.
